Section 4: Concurrency Control & State Consistency
Handling Simultaneous Claims: The system must gracefully manage situations
where multiple users attempt to take responsibility for the same work item at the
exact same time. The agent must implement a conditional database update (e.g.,
UPDATE work_items SET assignee = ? WHERE id = ? AND assignee IS
NULL
). If this query updates zero rows, the server must reject the action with a
409 Conflict to ensure exactly one user succeeds.
PDF + 2
Stale Data & Optimistic Locking: Users will inevitably view information that
another user has just changed. To prevent overriding concurrent edits, the agent
must implement optimistic locking using the version column. The client must
send the version it currently sees, and the backend must execute an update
strictly WHERE version = ? , incrementing the version upon success. A version
mismatch must immediately trigger a 409 Conflict .
PDF + 3
Idempotency for Retries: The system must handle users repeating an action
due to network uncertainty or double-clicking. The agent must require an
Idempotency-Key header for creation and state-mutating requests. The server
must store this key alongside its response and return the exact stored result if
the identical key is transmitted again, preventing duplicate item creation.
PDF + 2
Frontend Reconciliation: When the server rejects an action due to a conflict
(e.g., 409 or 403 ), the frontend application must cleanly roll back any
optimistic state updates and explicitly prompt the user to reload the stale data.
